Mike Marty is in Reinhold, Pennsylvania,

48
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,000
and he's the scoutmaster of Troop 214.

49
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:55,000
And he wrote to say thanks for the great site.

50
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:57,000
I've been browsing your daily emails for some time

51
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:01,000
and just recently started listening to the podcast as I drive to work.

52
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:03,000
I'm a scoutmaster for a new troop.

53
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,000
My involvement with scouting started years ago

54
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:09,000
when I took my oldest son to a role in Tigers

55
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:12,000
And I also signed up to be the Tiger Dan leader.

56
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:15,000
Both of my sons are in Scouts, working on their Eagle.

57
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:18,000
Both have autism, but they're very high-functioning.

58
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:20,000
This has been a bit of a challenge.

59
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,000
Sometimes I inflict those challenges on myself

60
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:25,000
when I try to protect them too much.

61
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,000
Recently my youngest son completed national youth leader training

62
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:30,000
and the OA ordeal.

63
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:34,000
What made this possible was my not being involved

64
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:36,000
and the Scouts and the adults in leadership

65
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:38,000
at those events having an open mind.

66
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,000
The majority of Scouts in our troop

67
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
are identified with one learning difference or another.

68
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:47,000
We work to reach out to boys who really need Scouts.

69
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:51,000
Once again, thanks for helping me be a better scout leader.

70
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:52,000
Thanks, Mike.

71
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:55,000
Thanks for the kind words and thanks for getting in touch.

72
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:58,000
Eric Humann writes to us from actually the north of France

73
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:00,000
and says thanks for the work you do

74
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:02,000
and for sharing your knowledge.

75
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:03,000
Well, thank you, Eric.

76
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,000
Thanks for being in touch.

77
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:10,000
Here's a response from the anonymous writer of an email

78
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:13,000
we talked about in podcast 192..

79
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:16,000
Thanks for your personal email response to my question

80
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,000
and your response on the podcast,

81
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:21,000
The distinction between objective and subjective requirements

82
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:24,000
and the point that we are looking for individual progress

83
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,000
is very helpful.

84
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,000
I'll encourage my troop committee and our advancement chair

85
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,000
to take a listen to the podcast and see what happens.

86
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:33,000
Thanks for all you do.

87
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:35,000
In fact, I really don't know how you do it.

88
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:37,000
You must be retired or independently wealthy

89
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:39,000
or have a very understanding wife.

90
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:41,000
Well, I'm not retired

91
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,000
and I'm not independently wealthy by any means.

92
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:46,000
but you hit the nail on the head,

93
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:49,000
I do have a very understanding wife.

94
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Thanks for getting in touch and thanks for your kind words.

95
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:55,000
As I've been talking about on the last few podcasts,

96
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:57,000
my new book, The Scouting Journey,

97
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:01,000
is on its own little journey to completion right now

98
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:05,000
And you know it takes more than just getting it all written.

99
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:07,000
It has to be proofed and all that stuff

100
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:09,000
And slowly but surely we're seeing the light

101
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:10,000
at the end of the tunnel

102
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:13,000
And our publication date is mid-November.

103
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:15,000
It looks like we're going to be on track.

104
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,000
so more news about that later.

105
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:19,000
On November 10th, which is a Sunday

106
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:22,000
from 9 to 10 pm Eastern Daylight Time,

107
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:26,000
we'll be doing our next Scout Circle at scoutscircleorg

108
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:30,000
And that'll be about the transition from Weebelows to Scout.

109
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Make sure to tune in and have a listen,

110
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:35,000
And in this podcast I have a story to tell you.

111
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:36,000
It's a good Halloween story,

112
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:39,000
And then we're going to answer some email questions.

113
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:43,000
That's going to constitute the remainder of this transmission.

114
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:45,000
So let's get started. shall we

115
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:50,000
Bride me a letter? send it by name

116
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:53,000
Email. that is, folks.

117
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:57,000
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

118
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:08,000
Brian Rodini asked this question.

119
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:11,000
We have several parents that do participate regularly

120
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:13,000
with the troop on camping events

121
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,000
and direct scouts in what feels like everything.

122
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
They're cooking duties, cleaning up, later

123
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:20,000
getting ready for a hike.

124
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:22,000
Their intentions are good, but they interfere a lot.

125
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:24,000
They tell scouts to move over and let them show you

126
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:26,000
how to do something, or that they'll take care

127
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:27,000
of putting the tents away.

128
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,000
Over the course of several years

129
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:32,000
I've spoken to a parent or two and the scout master has.

130
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:35,000
The committee chair has asked them please to step back

131
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:38,000
and enjoy the camp out and let the boys do what patrols do,

132
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
But it's not working very well.

133
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,000
So as the assistant scout master,

134
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,000
who is soon to become scout master,

135
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:47,000
I am able to interact and work with my scouts.

136
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:50,000
Trying to get the message to parents to stop interfering

137
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:53,000
with the scouts is another matter.

138
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:55,000
Brian, thanks for getting in touch.

139
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,000
I'll tell you that there's absolutely no question

140
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:59,000
that parents who are not registered volunteers

141
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:03,000
don't really belong on camping trips or in troop meetings.

142
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:06,000
Somehow things have gone astray here

143
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:08,000
and you're going to need to make that stop.

144
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:10,000
It's a simple fix really.

145
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:14,000
Tell parents, if they want to participate on a regular basis,

146
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:16,000
that they need to register as scout leaders

147
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:17,000
and complete their training.

148
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:19,000
Otherwise they're just not going to be welcome

149
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,000
on camping trips or at meetings,

150
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:24,000
unless they'd like to observe a meeting or a camping trip.

151
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:25,000
You know that's fine,

152
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:27,000
But there are rules.

153
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:29,000
Observers watch and they don't participate.

154
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:31,000
They don't coach or interfere

155
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:33,000
and they're going to need to learn to control themselves.

156
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:37,000
You can't talk parents out of interfering

157
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
because they don't see it as interfering.

158
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:42,000
You can train scouters to do things properly.

159
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:45,000
Now I'm not sure where everyone got the idea

160
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,000
that scout camping is some kind of family event.

161
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:49,000
It's not.

162
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:51,000
None of the parents who are interfering

163
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,000
with a scout camping trip would do something

164
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:55,000
like suit up and head out on the field

165
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:57,000
to play in their child's soccer game.

166
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:59,000
If they did, the referee would throw them off the field.

167
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:00,000
Remember this:

168
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:04,000
The scout master coaches and advises scouts

169
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:07,000
and they lead adults.

170
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:09,000
If adults won't follow the leader.

171
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:13,000
they really just can't be part of what's going on.

172
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:15,000
Get together with your committee chair

173
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:17,000
and come up with a strategy you can agree on

174
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:18,000
to address this,

175
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:20,000
And you're going to have to tell parents

176
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:22,000
in no uncertain terms that these things

177
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:24,000
are going to change.

178
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:27,000
I'll tell you what my general kind of standing order

179
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:28,000
is for adults.

180
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:31,000
One adult- the scout master, when he's present

181
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:34,000
and his designee when he's not,

182
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:37,000
interacts with and directs youth leadership

183
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:40,000
and scouts during meetings and camping trips.

184
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:43,000
No one else speaks to or directs

185
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,000
any youth leader or scout

186
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,000
without going through the scout master.

187
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,000
When scouts are meeting or camping,

188
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,000
that's game time.

189
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:53,000
No parents are allowed on the field.

190
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:55,000
No adult volunteers are allowed on the field.

191
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:58,000
They can observe and they can offer help,

192
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:00,000
but they're going to do that

193
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:02,000
by talking to the scout master.

194
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:04,000
When we go camping, there's an area for adults

195
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:06,000
at a good remove from the scouts,

196
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:09,000
within sight, but as many yards away as possible.

197
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,000
Adults do not go into the scouts area

198
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,000
unless they have my permission,

199
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:15,000
and no scout other than the senior patrol leader

200
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:17,000
enters the adult area.

201
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:19,000
This would be a very big change

202
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:20,000
from what you described.

203
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:22,000
It's going to take a little while to make it work

204
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:25,000
and it could cause a little bit of difficulty,

205
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,000
but you'll be equal to the challenge

206
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,000
if you gather together the resources

207
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,000
in the scout master handbook

208
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:33,000
and in the scout handbook and from your training

209
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,000
that delineate the role of parents

210
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
and the role of volunteers

211
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,000
and the role of youth leader.

212
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:41,000
I received this and it was requested

213
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:44,000
that I withhold the name of the person who sent it to me.

214
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:45,000
They wrote in to say this:

215
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,000
first of all, I love the podcast in the site.

216
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
I began listening six months ago

217
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
and I've now started from the beginning

218
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
and I'm working my way through the podcast archives.

219
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:55,000
The insight you share with your audience

220
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:57,000
is extremely useful

221
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,000
and it's fun to listen to.

222
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:01,000
I've enjoyed reading your recommendations

223
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:03,000
and read as much as I can find out about scouting,

224
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:05,000
its history and its methods.

225
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:07,000
I appreciate the kind words.

226
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:08,000
Here's my dilemma.

227
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:10,000
I'm an assistant scout master

228
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:12,000
with a group of about 60 scouts.

229
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:14,000
I came up through scouting

230
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:16,000
as a den leader, unit commissioner

231
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:18,000
and the father of a scout.

232
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:20,000
I've noticed in the six months

233
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,000
I've been involved with the troop

234
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:24,000
that there are some challenges

235
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:26,000
and a few red flags with the program.

236
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,000
Most all of the problems

237
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:30,000
stem from adults taking command

238
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,000
when they believe the meeting or event

239
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:34,000
is becoming chaotic

240
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
and they have a general feeling

241
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:38,000
that the boys can't lead

242
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,000
and they really care about the scouts

243
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,000
and want them to have the best experience possible,

244
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:44,000
but they seem to feel it requires

245
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:46,000
stepping in too much of the time.

246
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,000
The current scout master is retiring

247
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,000
and the committee is looking for a replacement.

248
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:52,000
I was asked to take the position

249
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,000
and under the circumstances

250
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:56,000
I would gladly jump into this volcano,

251
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:58,000
But for the next 18 months

252
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:00,000
I'm going to miss

253
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:02,000
about half of the troop meetings

254
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,000
because of a work commitment.

255
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,000
The committee discussed the conflict that I had

256
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:08,000
and they decided that, while it may be difficult,

257
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:10,000
they still wanted me to be scout master

258
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:12,000
and that other adults

259
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:14,000
could fill in when I am absent.

260
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,000
I appreciate their support

261
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:18,000
and their desire to compromise,

262
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:20,000
but I see this kind of scout master by committee

263
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:22,000
to be a disaster in the making.

264
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:24,000
I'm pretty sure I'd be able to coax the youth

265
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:26,000
and adults along towards the youth-led

266
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,000
patrol method style of troop

267
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,000
if I could be there full time,

