for the big campery campfire.

549
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,000
and this campfire was held

550
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
in this kind of wonderful natural

551
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
amphitheater we have at one of our

552
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
scout camps. there's a creek in the

553
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
background

554
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,000
and kind of the stage

555
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
is just right there

556
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,000
high on the bank of the creek

557
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
and then up the hill

558
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,000
near the creek there are benches

559
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
in this kind of nice natural

560
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,000
amphitheater.

561
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:26,000
John got into his pumpkin

562
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
man outfit

563
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:30,000
that day- glow orange jumpsuit-

564
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,000
and he got himself out of sight

565
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:34,000
down towards the creeks

566
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:36,000
and nobody could see him.

567
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:38,000
and you know, at the right

568
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,000
time the scout started to filter into

569
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,000
the campfire circle

570
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,000
and of course they brought their pumpkins.

571
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:46,000
and as the pumpkins came in we took

572
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,000
them and we sat them

573
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
around the campfire in a big

574
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:52,000
semi circle. it looked great

575
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,000
and we started the campfire

576
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:56,000
with a song and then it was time

577
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
for the first skit and what we

578
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,000
had done is we had this

579
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
log, this kind of a stump,

580
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
and we set that over

581
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
to one side of the campfire

582
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
and the idea was,

583
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
when your troop came

584
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
down to do a skit, we would take

585
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
your pumpkin right

586
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
and we would set it on the log

587
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:18,000
and we would say, hey, here's troop

588
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:20,000
15's pumpkin,

589
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:22,000
and what do you think of this pumpkin,

590
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,000
isn't it gorgeous? and everybody would go

591
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,000
yay, and then we would put it on the log

592
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,000
and they would come out and do their skit.

593
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:30,000
so the first troop came up,

594
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,000
we put the pumpkin on the log

595
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:34,000
and they came out and did their skit.

596
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:36,000
and of course you know I came out afterwards

597
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:38,000
and it was

598
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,000
naturally. it was one of those skits

599
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:42,000
that you've seen, you know, several hundred

600
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:44,000
times, right?

601
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:46,000
if you've been to more than one scout

602
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:48,000
campfire, you know exactly what I'm talking about.

603
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:50,000
there's seems to be,

604
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
by regulation, almost

605
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:54,000
a number of skits that must be part

606
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:56,000
of each and every campfire.

607
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:58,000
so this skit- you know it was

608
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:00,000
fine, they did a fine job

609
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:02,000
of it- is one that a lot of people had seen before,

610
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
and so after the skit

611
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
was over and the applause died down

612
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:08,000
a little bit, I stepped out and I said, well,

613
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
let's give a cheer

614
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,000
for that skit.

615
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:14,000
and I forget exactly what cheer we gave,

616
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:16,000
did a cheer and everything,

617
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,000
and I asked the audience.

618
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,000
I said, well, what did you really think of that skit?

619
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,000
and you know how scouts are

620
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
they're.

621
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:26,000
they told us their opinion and it was mixed.

622
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:28,000
and I said: you know,

623
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,000
I have a friend here

624
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:32,000
who also might have an opinion about this skit.

625
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:34,000
I said because it's close to Halloween,

626
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:36,000
we have all these jack-o'-lanterns here.

627
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:38,000
I invited my friend the pumpkin man

628
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:40,000
to come and be with us this evening

629
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:42,000
and I looked off

630
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:44,000
and I said: hello, pumpkin man.

631
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:46,000
pumpkin man, are you there?

632
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:48,000
and John kind of popped up

633
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:50,000
from behind the creek bank

634
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:52,000
in this crazy orange jumpsuit

635
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:54,000
and strode up to the campfire

636
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,000
nonchalantly and we shook hands

637
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:58,000
and I welcomed him to the campfire,

638
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:00,000
introduced him to the scouts and I said: so,

639
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:02,000
pumpkin man, did you,

640
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:04,000
did you see the last skit?

641
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:06,000
and he said, yeah, I saw the last skit.

642
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:08,000
and I said: wasn't it great?

643
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:10,000
didn't you think that was a great skit?

644
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:12,000
not really.

645
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:14,000
and I said: well, pumpkin man, please,

646
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:16,000
we're trying to be kind here-

647
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:18,000
are you sure you

648
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:20,000
didn't like the last skit?

649
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:22,000
he said, no, not really

650
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:24,000
seen that skit a lot of times.

651
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:26,000
I don't think they did very well.

652
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:28,000
and of course this was all coming off the cuff.

653
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:30,000
it was a joke and everybody understood

654
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:32,000
in the way that we were doing it,

655
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:34,000
so no feelings were hurt,

656
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:36,000
but I got to

657
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:38,000
honestly disagree with you, pumpkin man.

658
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:40,000
it was a great skit. and he said:

659
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:42,000
you want to know what I really thought of that skit.

660
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:44,000
and I said, yeah, that's why I brought you out,

661
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:46,000
I want you to give us your opinion.

662
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:48,000
he walked out of the campfire light

663
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:50,000
and he came back with-

664
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:52,000
have you guessed it yet?

665
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:54,000
good old John had spent part of the afternoon

666
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:56,000
gathering the materials

667
00:24:56,000 --> 00:24:58,000
and putting together

668
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:00,000
a giant mallet.

669
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:02,000
I mean, this thing was huge.

670
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:04,000
it was probably about four or five feet long.

671
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:06,000
it had a big log

672
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:08,000
12 inches in diameter as the mallet head

673
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:10,000
on it and he brought that out

674
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:12,000
and there was a little murmur

675
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:14,000
that started to go through the crowd

676
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:16,000
once he got a big hammer for it.

677
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:18,000
what's this all about?

678
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:20,000
and John looked at

679
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:22,000
the jack-o'-lantern

680
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:24,000
that was sitting on the log,

681
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:26,000
the troop who had just done

682
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:28,000
their skit- and he said:

683
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:30,000
so you really want me to tell you what I thought of that skit?

684
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:32,000
and once again I said

685
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:34,000
yeah, I really want you to tell you

686
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:36,000
that skit. he says that pumpkin there

687
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:38,000
that belongs to the guys who did the skit.

688
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:40,000
and I said yeah,

689
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:42,000
and the murmur in the crowd

690
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:44,000
started to go up a little bit now

691
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:46,000
and they started to get a little excited

692
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:48,000
because they saw the pumpkin

693
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:50,000
sitting there on the log.

694
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:52,000
they saw the pumpkin men

695
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:54,000
and they saw the giant mallet

696
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:56,000
and in their fevered imagination

697
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:58,000
they started to make

698
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:00,000
some connections.

699
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:02,000
the pumpkin man took a couple of strides over to the log

700
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:04,000
and he lifted up that mallet

701
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:06,000
and he brought it down with a resounding

702
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:08,000
crash

703
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:10,000
and smashed the pumpkin into a thousand pieces

704
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:12,000
pieces

705
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:14,000
and a roar

706
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:16,000
of approbation went up from the crowd

707
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:18,000
like none you've ever heard in a scout camp before.

708
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:20,000
they were thrilled.

709
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:22,000
the guys who made the jack-o'-lantern were thrilled.

710
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:24,000
the rest of the scouts were thrilled.

711
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:26,000
they thought it was the best thing they had ever seen.

712
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:28,000
and so the pumpkin man

713
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:30,000
took a bow and went off

714
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:32,000
stage again and it was time for

715
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:34,000
the next troop to come up and do their skit.

716
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:36,000
and dutifully,

717
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:38,000
as they took the stage,

718
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:40,000
I inquired as to which jack-o'-lantern was theirs

719
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:42,000
and I put that on the stump

720
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:44,000
where they now smashed jack-o'-lantern

721
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:46,000
used to sit

722
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:48,000
and they went ahead and they did their skit.

723
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:50,000
and at the end of the skit

724
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:52,000
I said: well, what did you guys think of that skit?

725
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:54,000
you know how

726
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:56,000
scouts are. they were calling

727
00:26:56,000 --> 00:26:58,000
and carrying on

728
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:00,000
and I said: shall we ask the pumpkin man

729
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:02,000
what he thought of the skit?

730
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:04,000
yes, ask the pumpkin man.

731
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:06,000
so John strides back out

732
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:08,000
and I said, pumpkin man, what did you really think of that skit?

733
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:10,000
he says, well, it wasn't too bad.

734
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:12,000
it was a little bit better than the last one.

735
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:14,000
and I still don't like it very much.

736
00:27:14,000 --> 00:27:16,000
and I said now, pumpkin man, come on,

737
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:18,000
they were doing their best.

738
00:27:18,000 --> 00:27:20,000
they came out and they gave us a great skit,

739
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:22,000
don't you think that? nah,

740
00:27:22,000 --> 00:27:24,000
I really didn't like that skit.

741
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:26,000
and he looks at the crowd and he says,

742
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:28,000
yes, yes, we want to know.

743
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:30,000
and so he strides over to the stump

744
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:32,000
where the poor innocent jack-o'-lantern

745
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:34,000
is sitting

746
00:27:34,000 --> 00:27:36,000
and he lifts his mallet up over his head

747
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:38,000
and he brings it down and he stops

748
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:40,000
and he puts the mallet down

749
00:27:40,000 --> 00:27:42,000
and he rubs his hands together,

750
00:27:42,000 --> 00:27:44,000
he spits into his palms

751
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:46,000
and he picks the mallet up again,

752
00:27:46,000 --> 00:27:48,000
brings it up over his head,

753
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:50,000
he brings it crashing down on that pumpkin

754
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:52,000
and it goes, blow into smithereens

755
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:54,000
all over the place.

756
00:27:54,000 --> 00:27:56,000
the crowd goes wild again

757
00:27:56,000 --> 00:27:58,000
and it was time then

758
00:27:58,000 --> 00:28:00,000
to bring the third troop up for their skit.

759
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:02,000
and we found their pumpkin

760
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:04,000
and we put it on the stump

761
00:28:04,000 --> 00:28:06,000
where the poor innocent pumpkins

762
00:28:06,000 --> 00:28:08,000
that had been smashed to the thus far head-sat

763
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:10,000
and this troop went ahead

764
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:12,000
and got into a skit

765
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:14,000
and they got a couple minutes in

766
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:16,000
and pretty soon the crowd was chanting:

767
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:18,000
pumpkin man,

768
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:20,000
pumpkin man,

769
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:22,000
pumpkin man,

770
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:24,000
and you know,

771
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:26,000
it was almost impossible to stop them.

772
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:28,000
it was a runaway train

773
00:28:28,000 --> 00:28:30,000
and of course, out comes the pumpkin man,

774
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:32,000
down comes the hammer

775
00:28:32,000 --> 00:28:34,000
and another jack-o'-lantern smashed.

776
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:36,000
well, we had

777
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:38,000
a fantastic time that evening.

778
00:28:38,000 --> 00:28:40,000
it was one of the best campfires

779
00:28:40,000 --> 00:28:42,000
I've ever been to,

780
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:44,000
oft commented on, and everybody

781
00:28:44,000 --> 00:28:46,000
had a blast and we smashed

782
00:28:46,000 --> 00:28:48,000
15 or 20 pumpkins

783
00:28:48,000 --> 00:28:50,000
and everybody thought it was just

784
00:28:50,000 --> 00:28:52,000
wonderful.

785
00:28:52,000 --> 00:28:54,000
now that would be enough of a story to tell,

786
00:28:54,000 --> 00:28:56,000
but let me dial ahead a year or so.

787
00:28:56,000 --> 00:28:58,000
John and I

788
00:28:58,000 --> 00:29:00,000
end up down at camp for one reason or another-

789
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:02,000
I really can't remember why,

790
00:29:02,000 --> 00:29:04,000
and I'm talking to him and we're just

791
00:29:04,000 --> 00:29:06,000
kind of catching up- and he said

792
00:29:06,000 --> 00:29:08,000
that thing at the camperee that went over great.

793
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:10,000
and I said, yeah, the pumpkin man.

794
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:12,000
I said that was fantastic.

795
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:14,000
he said yep, yep.

796
00:29:14,000 --> 00:29:16,000
last month the pumpkin man came

797
00:29:16,000 --> 00:29:18,000
to visit the Cub Scout camp

798
00:29:18,000 --> 00:29:20,000
and I said, John,

799
00:29:20,000 --> 00:29:22,000
you didn't, did you?

800
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:24,000
he said yeah, it didn't turn out

801
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:26,000
to be a very good idea, though.

802
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:30,000
I said, John what happened?

803
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:32,000
he said: well, we did the same thing.

804
00:29:32,000 --> 00:29:34,000
we told all the dens to make a jack-o'-lantern

805
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:36,000
and bring it with them to the campfire,

806
00:29:36,000 --> 00:29:38,000
and we did the same thing.

807
00:29:38,000 --> 00:29:40,000
their mothers got kind of angry

808
00:29:40,000 --> 00:29:42,000
with us, though, because they spent

809
00:29:42,000 --> 00:29:44,000
an awful long time working on those jack-o'-lanterns,

810
00:29:44,000 --> 00:29:48,000
and so from that

811
00:29:48,000 --> 00:29:50,000
day forward, at least as far

812
00:29:50,000 --> 00:29:52,000
as Cubs were concerned, the pumpkin man

813
00:29:52,000 --> 00:29:54,000
went into retirement.

